from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

UML = """classDiagram
    class Printable {
        <<interface>>
        +render() String
    }
    class Invoice {
        +int number
        +float total
        +render() String
    }
    class Photo {
        +String name
        +render() String
    }
    Printable <|.. Invoice
    Printable <|.. Photo
"""


class Printable(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def render(self):
        """Return the text to print."""


class Invoice(Printable):
    def __init__(self, number, total):
        self.number = number
        self.total = total

    def render(self):
        return f"Invoice #{self.number}: {self.total}"


class Photo(Printable):
    def __init__(self, name, width, height):
        self.name = name
        self.width = width
        self.height = height

    def render(self):
        return f"Photo {self.name} ({self.width}x{self.height})"


def print_all(items):
    return [item.render() for item in items]

explanation:
**Interface and realization.** The interface lists *what* can be done (`render()`); each class decides *how*. Callers depend on the interface only, so new classes plug in without changing them.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class Printable {
        <<interface>>
        +render() String
    }
    class Invoice {
        +render() String
    }
    class Photo {
        +render() String
    }
    Printable <|.. Invoice
    Printable <|.. Photo
```

**Dotted versus solid.** Solid triangle line = inheritance (class from class). Dotted triangle line = realization (class implements interface). In Python both are written `class X(Base)`; in C# and Java they are different keywords (`: IPrintable` versus `: Base`).

**Why the test adds `Receipt`.** If `print_all` works for a class it has never seen, the dependency really is on the interface.
